- op: discuss  # 78b8
  at: '2026-06-02T04:37:45Z'  # 78b8
  by: agent  # 78b8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 78b8
  clock: 4  # 78b8
  nonce: 78b8  # 78b8
  message: "**Pass-23 substrate-verified faces (F23.2, F23.3, F23.4, F23.5).** Pass 23 ran a \"claim-vs-substrate verification\" probe on surfaces touched by the INV-sugat closure campaign. Four new INV-luhur faces surfaced beyond the existing children:\n\n**(F23.2) `AnalysisRun.toolchain.name = \"python\"` for ALL 84 ARs, including grammar-based passes.** Distinct from WI-mipul (which is about ARs lacking the toolchain extension at all): this is about the value-semantic of `toolchain.name` even when populated. `rust` AR has `toolchain={'grammar_module': 'tree_sitter_rust', 'name': 'python', ...}`; `swift` AR has `grammar_module='tree_sitter_swift'` but `name='python'`. Field NAME suggests analyzer-language; VALUE is runtime. Same sub-pattern (3) \"same name, two definitions across paths\" as INV-pubom/INV-mozaf, but at the AR-toolchain layer. Either rename `toolchain.name → toolchain.runtime_name` + add `toolchain.analyzer_name`, or change the writer to record analyzer language.\n\n**(F23.3) `profile.languages` and `metrics.languages` carry disjoint language sets.** Symmetric difference of 4 across two writers: profile.languages = `{xml, yaml, ...}` (no ansible, no unknown); metrics.languages = `{ansible, unknown, ...}` (no xml, no yaml). The yaml/ansible mismatch suggests one writer reclassifies YAML files as Ansible when they parse as playbooks; the other doesn't. The \"unknown\" entry in metrics (23 edges, 0 nodes, 0 files) means metrics rolled up edges whose src language couldn't be classified — profile, walking files directly, never sees this category. Two writers (`profile.compute_profile` and `metrics.compute_metrics`) disagree on the language set. Closely related to WI-jikik schema-consolidation gap: profile.languages has `{files, loc}` per language; metrics.languages has `{nodes, edges}` per language; fields are complementary, but sets are also disjoint.\n\n**(F23.4) AR list out of `started_at` order on 21/83 (25%) transitions.** Iterating analysis_runs in document order does not yield chronological order. First 10 ARs show pattern: html → go → csharp (earlier start) → rust → swift → javascript (earlier start) → play-routes → sql (earlier start) → yaml_ansible → xml. Likely completion-order serialization from parallel execution; no documented contract either way. Cosmetic but audit-impactful — first-time consumers expect alphabetical, topological, or chronological. Either document the contract or sort at serialization time.\n\n**(F23.5) `validation_report.schema_version='0.1'` vs `bm.schema_version='0.13.0'` — undocumented sub-schema versioning.** The new validation_report section (added by Phase 0) carries its own independent schema_version field. The relationship between the two version timelines is undocumented. A consumer reading bm.schema_version=0.13.0 cannot determine the validation_report's sub-schema version from any external mapping. If validation_report's structure changes (e.g., violation record format), consumers using bm.schema_version as the validity gate get silent breakage. Either consolidate to a single version, or document the relationship (in spec.md or as a top-level `sub_schemas` mapping).\n\nAll four are face-level extensions of INV-luhur. No new tracker items filed for them individually; this discussion entry is the record. They will surface in WI-mipul's tail or future related WIs as appropriate.\n\n**Lab notebook entry:** `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_06022026_0010_pass23.md`."  # 78b8
- op: discuss  # 37bc
  at: '2026-06-02T05:21:53Z'  # 37bc
  by: agent  # 37bc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 37bc
  clock: 5  # 37bc
  nonce: 37bc  # 37bc
  message: "Pass-25 cross-format consistency probe — three new writer-naming-asymmetry faces in the AR / meta-layer:\n\nF25.5 (quantifies INV-janob's existing finding): AR.origin_run_id is 100% NULL on all 84 ARs; AR.execution_id is the populated identity (84/84). Node `origin_run_id` and edge `origin_run_id` are FK names — they correctly map to AR.execution_id values (109915/109915 edges resolve; 32025/34253 nodes resolve; the remaining 2228 are the '' sentinel cohort, see INV-virik F25.4). Field-name disagreement at producer (`execution_id`) vs consumer (`origin_run_id`) surfaces, exactly the INV-janob shape.\n\nF25.13 (new naming-asymmetry face): AR.toolchain.grammar_module values (30 distinct, all `language_pack:<grammar>` form — e.g. language_pack:apex, language_pack:bash, language_pack:gleam) and reproducibility_context.captured.grammars keys (51 distinct, all `tree-sitter-<grammar>` form — e.g. tree-sitter-agda, tree-sitter-bash, tree-sitter-cpp) have **0 overlap**. Same tree-sitter grammar identity at two surfaces with two naming conventions. Same shape as pass-23 F23.3 (profile.languages vs metrics.languages disjoint vocabulary): one fact, two formats. Either the convention should be unified, or one surface should provide a translation layer.\n\nF25.14 (methodological observation): The AR record's JSON field for the analyzer name is `pass` (a Python keyword!), but the conceptual term in spec / agent-notes / INV-numat F24.1 / pass_id rows is `pass_id`. Earlier pass-25 probes (Q5, Q28) returned None for all 84 ARs because `ar.get('pass_id')` silently fails. Field-name vs concept-name asymmetry, same family as INV-janob. The cost of this asymmetry is concrete: consumers writing probes against the conceptual name get vacuous results (no exception raised) and may draw silent-wrong conclusions. Recommendation: rename either the JSON field or the conceptual term so the canonical name is unambiguous.\n\nAll three are \"same fact, two names\" patterns at different layers of the meta-layer — INV-luhur's writer-contract META scope subsumes them."  # 37bc
- op: discuss  # a24a
  at: '2026-06-02T05:47:41Z'  # a24a
  by: agent  # a24a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a24a
  clock: 6  # a24a
  nonce: a24a  # a24a
  message: "Pass-26 provenance-chain + producer-consumer probes — three substantive faces + one methodological reframing of pass-25 findings:\n\nF26.1 (metrics.languages derivation rule undocumented): Σ(nodes by node.language) for python/javascript/typescript is LESS than metrics.languages.<lang>.nodes by delta = 262 nodes. Cross-check: 262 nodes have node.language=None. The 262 are all linker-emitted synthetic nodes (subprocess-linker 78, message-queue-linker 75, database-query-linker 34, websocket-linker 30, http-linker 18, event-sourcing-linker 14, graphql-resolver-linker 11, js-module-linker 2). So metrics.languages applies a per-node attribution rule that includes language=None nodes against specific languages — the rule isn't documented in spec or schema. python=33703 in metrics but 33455 by node.language (+248 from linkers emitting python-host synthetic functions); javascript=13 in metrics but 2 by node.language (+11); typescript=67 in metrics but 64 by node.language (+3).\n\nF26.6 (profile.lang.loc and Σ(node.lines_of_code) measure different quantities): profile.languages.python.loc=481203 vs Σ(non-file python node lines_of_code)=801588 (1.66× difference). Cause is nested-symbol double-counting in the Σ direction: class `TreeSitterAnalyzer` has loc=1079 and 23 methods that sum to 888 — methods are counted both individually AND within the class's range. file-kind node.lines_of_code=0 for python (file analyzer doesn't populate file LOC); profile.python.loc=481203 is computed independently via a separate path. Net consequence: there's no internal way to verify profile.lang.loc against substrate — it's an unaccountable external quantity. Same for markdown/yaml/json/toml/sql/xml/wgsl/solidity/html where profile reports thousands of LOC but Σ(node.LOC)=0 because those analyzers don't populate node-level LOC at all.\n\nF26.15 (pass-25 F25.5 / F25.14 reframing — methodological): Both pass-25 findings overstated structural issues that are actually intentional, documented design.\n\n- F25.14 framed the JSON-field `pass` vs concept term `pass_id` as a naming-asymmetry trap. Per spec.md line 272 (\"pass_id: str # e.g., \\\"python\\\" (serialized as \\\"pass\\\" in JSON output).\") and line 717 (\"**Output-specific note:** The IR field `pass_id` is serialized as `pass` in JSON output.\"), the alias is documented intentional design. ir.py:275 emits `\"pass\": self.pass_id` deliberately. Pass-25's \"rename the field or concept\" recommendation was unnecessary — the alias is canonical. The probe-debug cost (calling `ar.get('pass_id')` returns None silently for 84/84 ARs) is real but is a probe-author concern, not a structural bug.\n\n- F25.5 framed AR.origin_run_id as \"100% NULL on all 84 ARs\" — but AR does not have an `origin_run_id` field at all. The dataclass (ir.py:194-217) only has `execution_id` (and `pass_id`, `version`, `run_signature`, `repo_fingerprint`, `toolchain`, `config_fingerprint`, files_*, skipped_*, failed_*, warnings, started_at, duration_ms, pass_version). spec.md line 1776 explicitly documents: \"nodes[].origin_run_id: String, references analysis_runs[].execution_id\" — the producer-side identity and consumer-side FK are named differently because they play different roles (database-style identity vs reference naming). The \"100% NULL\" measurement is actually \"field absent from record\"; pass-25 used `.get()` which silently returns None for missing keys, conflating the two.\n\nBoth reframings have a common root: Python's `.get('field', default=None)` returns None for both \"field present and explicitly None\" AND \"field absent from record\". Future cross-format probes should distinguish: `'field' in record` vs `record.get('field') is None`. Pass-25 used the wrong test in two places; pass-26 found this by grepping spec.md before drawing structural conclusions.\n\nImplications: INV-janob (the parent naming-asymmetry framing for F25.5) may need scope rewording — the asymmetry IS documented as design. The remaining INV-janob concern is whether a *consumer* migrating between bm.json versions needs translation help (a separate concern from \"is the asymmetry a bug\"). Pass-25's filing on INV-luhur for F25.14 stands as a probe-methodology observation but not a structural claim.\n\nF26.13 (positive confirmation of WI-mipul scope): pass_version='' cohort is EXACTLY WI-mipul's 14-target set — csharp, enclosure-linker, go, html, java, javascript, json, make, manifest_targets, play-routes, python, toml, xml, yaml_ansible. Set equality verified (∩=14, A\\B=∅, B\\A=∅). WI-mipul's scope holds without revision. Pass-26 adds: pass_version='' is the **empty-string sentinel** form (consistent with F25.4 origin_run_id='', F24.2 partial_results_reason=''). All three are reserved-empty-as-empty-string anti-patterns; the schema declares the field with str type and the producer writes '' rather than omitting."  # a24a
- op: discuss  # d85c
  at: '2026-06-02T06:28:26Z'  # d85c
  by: agent  # d85c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d85c
  clock: 7  # d85c
  nonce: d85c  # d85c
  message: "Pass-28 INV-luhur face — spec.md drift on the supply_chain object's documented field set.\n\n**Spec.md line 783** documents the JSON `supply_chain` object as:\n\n> Compiled from the IR's flat `supply_chain_tier` and `supply_chain_reason` fields into a nested object with an added `tier_name` field. [example shows `{\"tier\": 1, \"tier_name\": \"first_party\", \"reason\": \"matches ^src/\"}`]\n\nThis narrative names 3 sub-fields: `tier`, `tier_name`, `reason`. The example JSON also shows 3 fields.\n\n**Substrate truth**: `supply_chain` object has **8** sub-fields:\n```\n['is_config_file', 'is_example_file', 'is_exported', 'is_generated_file',\n 'is_test_file', 'reason', 'tier', 'tier_name']\n```\n\nFive fields (`is_test_file`, `is_example_file`, `is_config_file`, `is_generated_file`, `is_exported`) are declared as flat top-level dataclass fields in ir.py (lines 413-417) but serialized inside the `supply_chain` object by `Symbol.to_dict()` (ir.py:457-465). Spec.md doesn't document this 5-field relocation.\n\nThis is a different drift mechanism from pass-25 F25.13's grammar-naming asymmetry (which was two surfaces using different naming conventions). This is \"spec narrative describes 3 sub-fields, substrate has 8 sub-fields, 5 are undocumented at the supply_chain section\". Same family as F26.15 (substrate behaves per code; spec narrative lags).\n\nAlso: spec.md lines 254-264 show an abbreviated Symbol dataclass display that elides ~18 fields (cyclomatic_complexity, lines_of_code, signature, docstring, modifiers, discovery_language, protocol_origin, display_label, qualified_name, stable_id, shape_id, fingerprint, meta, is_test_file, is_example_file, is_config_file, is_generated_file, is_exported). The display only includes id, name, kind, language, path, span, origin, origin_run_id, supply_chain_tier, supply_chain_reason, quality. The abbreviation hides which fields exist in the dataclass at all — readers reading the Symbol declaration in spec.md see ~11 fields; the dataclass has ~28.\n\nImplications:\n- Consumers reading spec.md to know what's in the substrate get an incomplete picture for both Symbol top-level and supply_chain nested.\n- Pass-28 F28.1 (WI-bujot reframing) traces a real probe-error to spec.md being incomplete: if the spec mentioned `supply_chain.is_exported` in its sub-field list, the WI-bujot probe writer would have known to check there.\n- The drift is structural, not editorial: the ir.py code is the authority, spec.md narrative is downstream and stale.\n\nStatus: keep INV-luhur as `violated`. The \"AnalysisRun + behavior-map meta-layer writers have no validator\" framing covers this; pass-28 documents another instance of writer-vs-doc drift at the spec-narrative layer.\n\nProbe artifact: /tmp/pass28_probe1.py + /tmp/pass28_probe2.py + spec.md lines 254-264 + 783."  # d85c
- op: discuss  # ec9c
  at: '2026-06-02T06:40:39Z'  # ec9c
  by: agent  # ec9c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ec9c
  clock: 8  # ec9c
  nonce: ec9c  # ec9c
  message: "Pass-29 INV-luhur — complete schema-walk vs substrate-walk reconciliation table (Symbol + Edge + AnalysisRun).\n\nPass-28 F28.1 exposed the WI-bujot reframing through serializer inspection. Pass-29 builds the complete reconciliation: every dataclass-declared field for Symbol, Edge, AnalysisRun → serialization rule → substrate location → presence rate. 61 fields total; 14 (23%) have a non-trivial serialization rule.\n\n**Symbol** (29 dataclass fields → 29 substrate slots, 7 with non-trivial rule)\n\n| Field                 | Rule              | Substrate location              | Valued% |\n|-----------------------|-------------------|---------------------------------|---------|\n| supply_chain_tier     | relocate + rename | supply_chain.tier               | 100% |\n| supply_chain_reason   | relocate + rename | supply_chain.reason             | 100% |\n| is_test_file          | relocate          | supply_chain.is_test_file       | 75.7% True |\n| is_example_file       | relocate          | supply_chain.is_example_file    | 0% True (path-pattern absent) |\n| is_config_file        | relocate          | supply_chain.is_config_file     | 0.2% True |\n| is_generated_file     | relocate          | supply_chain.is_generated_file  | 0% True (path-pattern absent) |\n| is_exported           | relocate          | supply_chain.is_exported        | 25.7% True (this was F28.1) |\n| (22 other fields)     | direct            | <same name>                     | varies |\n\nNote: spec.md line 783 documents 3 supply_chain sub-fields {tier, tier_name, reason}; substrate has 8 (the 3 documented + 5 booleans relocated above). The undocumented 5 are confirmed all serialized from the dataclass via ir.py:457-465.\n\n**Edge** (17 dataclass fields → 14 substrate slots, 6 with non-trivial rule)\n\n| Field           | Rule                | Substrate location      | Presence |\n|-----------------|---------------------|-------------------------|----------|\n| edge_type       | rename              | type                    | 100% present |\n| evidence_type   | relocate-into-meta (always) | meta.evidence_type | 100% present |\n| evidence_lang   | relocate-into-meta (conditional, default None) | meta.evidence_lang | **0% present** — F28.3 |\n| evidence_spans  | relocate-into-meta (conditional, default None) | meta.evidence_spans | **0% present** — F28.3 |\n| dst_ref         | conditional-direct (only if not None) | dst_ref | 11.1% present |\n| derived_from    | conditional-direct (only if not None) | derived_from | 21.6% present |\n| (11 other fields) | direct | <same name>     | varies |\n\nThe conditional-write absence sub-pattern (10, added pass-28) applies cleanly to 4 Edge fields. ExternalRef.to_dict (ir.py:554) produces {lang, module_path, name} — substrate verified.\n\n**AnalysisRun** (15 dataclass fields → 15 substrate slots, 1 with rename)\n\n| Field    | Rule    | Substrate location | Presence |\n|----------|---------|---------------------|----------|\n| pass_id  | rename  | pass               | 100% present (F26.15 documented design) |\n| (14 other fields) | direct | <same name> | varies |\n\n**Summary of non-trivial rules:**\n- 7 Symbol fields with relocate/rename rules (all into supply_chain)\n- 6 Edge fields with rename / relocate-into-meta / conditional rules\n- 1 AR field with rename (pass_id → pass)\n- **Total: 14 of 61 (23%)** dataclass fields have a serialization rule that requires reading ir.py to_dict to predict the substrate location.\n\n**Spec.md documentation coverage of the 14 non-trivial rules:**\n- spec.md line 272 documents `pass_id → pass` ✓\n- spec.md line 717 documents `pass_id → pass` (output-specific note) ✓\n- spec.md line 783 documents `supply_chain_tier → supply_chain.tier` and `supply_chain_reason → supply_chain.reason` ✓ (partial — the 5 boolean relocations are not in line 783's description)\n- spec.md line 799 documents `evidence_type` / `evidence_lang` / `evidence_spans` → meta ✓ (does not flag the conditional-write absence behavior)\n- spec.md line 803 documents `edge_key` format ✓\n- spec.md does NOT document: `edge_type → type` rename (used in line 793 but not flagged as rename); `is_test_file` / `is_example_file` / `is_config_file` / `is_generated_file` / `is_exported` relocation into supply_chain; `dst_ref` / `derived_from` conditional behavior.\n\n**Coverage:** 4 of 14 non-trivial rules are documented in spec.md (29%). The remaining 10 rules (71%) require reading ir.py to_dict / from_dict to know about.\n\nThis is the structural artifact INV-luhur's umbrella implies. Fix-path candidate: spec.md §6 (\"Internal representation\") or §9 (\"Behavior map JSON\") could add a per-rule table; OR the dataclass docstring could note \"(serialized as <X>)\" inline; OR a generated `docs/schema.json` could carry the full serialization mapping.\n\nProbe artifact: /tmp/pass29_probe1.py / output at /tmp/pass29_output1.log. The methodology (probe1.py) is reusable for any future schema-vs-substrate audit.\n\nNo status change. Pass-29 supplies the structural inventory for the documentation drift INV-luhur umbrellas; the spec-side fix is human-decision territory."  # ec9c
