  message: "Pass-30 spec.md drift findings — batched 7 sub-faces (F30.1, F30.2, F30.3, F30.5, F30.6, F30.7, F30.8, F30.10, F30.13, F30.14).\n\nPass 30 ran two-way schema audit walking spec.md §6 + §9 against the substrate at /tmp/hg-dogfood-s21/bm.json. Sub-pattern (4) \"two-analyzer-cohorts-two-surfaces\" applied to spec-narrative-vs-substrate at scale.\n\n**Top-level scheme-identifier drift (probe 1.A).**\n- spec §9.667 example shows schema_version='0.2.1'; substrate has '0.13.0' (4 major versions out of date in spec narrative). Beyond WI-foful's stableid v2→v5 + canonical_name removal scope.\n- spec §6.373 documents stable_id_scheme='hypergumbo-stableid-v2'; substrate has 'hypergumbo-stableid-v5'. (Known.)\n- bm.symbol_fingerprint_scheme='hypergumbo-symbol-fp-v1' is present in substrate but NOT documented in spec §6.373-375 (which lists only 3 schemes). 4th scheme exists with its own writer at top-level. **Bonus reframing of pass-27 F27.6**: F27.6 said \"bm.fingerprint_scheme=None top-level reserved-empty\"; substrate ACTUALLY has the key absent entirely (not None-valued), AND spec doesn't declare a bare `fingerprint_scheme` field. F27.6 was a probe error (.get() returning None for absent key, same finding-family as F28.1 / F26.15 / F27.5). Real state: 4 prefixed scheme fields, all populated; no `fingerprint_scheme` (bare) exists or is reserved.\n\n**Top-level keys (probe 1.B).** 22 actual top-level keys vs 19 documented in §9 example block. 3 undocumented: supply_chain_summary, symbol_fingerprint_scheme (above), validation_report. validation_report is INV-sugat's artifact; supply_chain_summary is ADR-0024 territory. Neither appears in the §9.664-682 top-level structure example.\n\n**Edge.evidence_type vocabulary drift (probe 1.E).** Spec §9.825-834 lists 9 values; substrate has 33 distinct (24 undocumented + 9 documented − 4 unused). **27.1% of edges (29795/109915) use undocumented values.** Top undocumented by edge count: module_attribute_reference, enclosing_scope, import_resolution, import_statement, ast_call_inferred, ast_import. Documented-but-absent: ast_getattr_call, import_dynamic, script_inline, script_src. Spec §9.835 acknowledges \"the registry...is the live source of truth\" but the §9 narrative is the consumer-facing list and is materially stale.\n\n**Edge.type vocabulary drift (probe 1.F).** Spec §9.842-852 lists 8 canonical values; substrate has 18 distinct (13 undocumented + 8 documented − 3 unused). Undocumented in spec narrative: contains, decorated_by, depends_on, depends_on_manifest, dispatches_to, event_publishes, inherits, module_attr_ref, module_exports, overrides, references, script_src, uses. Documented-but-absent: defines_target, manual, renders. (`renders` is checkmarked ✅ but codebase has no template files so the absence is design-correct.)\n\n**feature record shape drift (probe 1.G).**\n- feature.summary documented in §9.856 but ABSENT from all 16 substrate features. Producer never emits this field; consumers reading spec would expect it.\n- feature.node_depths, feature.node_tiers are present in substrate but UNDOCUMENTED in spec §9.856.\n- feature.query has 5 undocumented sub-fields: exclude_imports, exclude_utility, hub_threshold, pass_through_kinds, reverse. (Spec lists only method, entrypoint, hops, max_files, exclude_tests.)\n- Same shape replicates in `hypergumbo slice` JSON output (slice subcommand emits same feature.query shape as bm.json features[].query).\n\n**Edge.meta documented-but-100%-absent (probe 1.H).** Spec §9.811-822 documents 15 meta sub-fields; substrate has 22 distinct. 100% absent from substrate: receiver, evidence[] (multi-pass), io_boundary, io_primitive, taint_labels, taint_sanitized_by. Of these:\n- taint_labels / taint_sanitized_by are conditional-design (only when verify-claims runs taint propagation) — design-correct.\n- evidence[], receiver documented as available; substrate 0%.\n- io_boundary / io_primitive (ADR-0016) spec §9.818 says \"Stamped on every call edge whose target is...a primitive.\" Substrate-side stamping is 0%. **`hypergumbo io-boundaries` works regardless** (shows 847 boundary calls) because the CLI recomputes on-the-fly by matching edge target paths against io_primitives YAML registry. Sub-pattern (NEW, pass-30): **CLI recomputes when substrate stamping missing** — consumer compensates for writer-contract gap, masking the absence at end-user surface. Likely applies to any CLI surface that can compute a field from substrate + external config.\n\nSame finding-family as pass-28 F28.3 (evidence_lang / evidence_spans 0% absent via conditional-write suppression).\n\n**supply_chain_summary internal schema inconsistency (probe 2.H / F30.10).** Substrate has 4 tiers in supply_chain_summary:\n- first_party: {files: 914, symbols: 32531}\n- internal_dep: {files: 1, symbols: 486}\n- external_dep: {files: 10, symbols: 1236}\n- **derived_skipped: {files: 0, paths: []}**\n\nderived_skipped uses field name `paths` (list[str]) where the other 3 tiers use `symbols` (int count). Different count-axis field per tier. The 4 tier sub-records have inconsistent shape — writer-contract divergence across enum-tier values, undocumented in spec.\n\n**Node.meta has 42 undocumented sub-keys (F30.14, probe 3 follow-up).** Spec §9 documents Edge.meta sub-fields extensively but does NOT document Node.meta at all (except `concepts` indirectly via §8.573). Substrate Node.meta has 42 distinct sub-keys; ~41 are undocumented in spec.md narrative. Distribution (top 14):\n\n```\n16968 parameters       3200 concepts          1638 external_boundary\n 1009 decorators        507 nesting_parent     216 base_classes\n  156 framework_role    130 call_kind           78 call_type\n   78 executable         78 is_python_m         78 subcommand\n   75 message_type       75 queue_type\n```\n\nLargest single spec-drift surface of pass 30. Producer emits 42-field meta vocabulary on nodes; spec narrative documents 1.\n\n**Coverage tally.** Of 14 substantive spec-narrative claims about substrate structure that pass 30 measured against substrate, 4 MATCH cleanly (shape_id_scheme, repo_fingerprint_scheme, confidence_model, view) + 1 §9.781 presence-rule HOLDS exactly (stable_id/shape_id key always present). 9 of 14 DRIFT (substantive divergence between spec narrative and substrate observation). DRIFT rate: 64% (9/14) of spec-narrative claims measured.\n\nSpec.md §9 is currently a substantially-stale snapshot of the substrate shape. The dataclass layer is more current (CI-enforced via `scripts/generate-schema --check`), but the narrative isn't gated; pass-29 F29.1 showed dataclass-narrative drift; pass-30 shows the substrate-narrative drift is wider. Fix-path candidate (human-decision): a generated §9 from `docs/schema.json`, OR a CI check that walks §9 string-literal claims against schema.json.\n\nNo status change. Pass-30 supplies the structural inventory for INV-luhur's spec-drift face at scale across §6 and §9.\n\nProbe artifacts: /tmp/pass30_probe1.py (20 sub-probes) + /tmp/pass30_probe2.py + /tmp/pass30_probe2b.py + /tmp/pass30_probe2c.py. Outputs in /tmp/pass30_output*.log."  # ca99
- op: discuss  # 8f71
  at: '2026-06-02T07:32:37Z'  # 8f71
  by: agent  # 8f71
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8f71
  clock: 10  # 8f71
  nonce: 8f71  # 8f71
  message: "Pass-31 spec-drift batch — 4 findings (F31.2 production-functions derivation undocumented; F31.3 verify-claims schema undocumented; F31.6 sketch --input perf claim doesn't hold; F31.7 subcommand performance profile).\n\n**F31.2 — \"Production functions: 4342\" is undocumented derivation shared across test-coverage and dead-code-maybe.** Both subcommands report identical figure (test-coverage: \"Total functions: 4342, Tested: 2579 (59.4%), Untested: 1763\"; dead-code-maybe: \"Production functions: 4342, Reachable: 451, Potentially dead: 3891\"). Substrate-derived candidates:\n- function-kind nodes total: 7098\n- function-kind first_party: 7098\n- function-kind first_party non-test: 3428\n- function+method first_party non-test: ~7818\nNone match 4342 exactly. Two subcommands evidently share an internal \"production functions\" filter rule. The rule is not in spec.md §6 / §9 / §10 / §11 narrative; not in `test-coverage --help`; not in `dead-code-maybe --help`. Consumer reading either subcommand cannot derive the substrate filter.\n\nFix-path candidate (human-decision): document the \"production functions\" rule in spec.md or at minimum in --help for both subcommands; consider exposing the filter as a CLI flag for reproducibility.\n\n**F31.3 — verify-claims YAML schema entirely undocumented in spec.md narrative.** Accepted YAML fields (from packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/verify_claims.py:188-228 load_claims()):\n```yaml\nschema_version: 1\nclaims:\n  - id: <str>\n    text: <str>\n    constraint:\n      boundary: <str>  # I/O boundary name\n      must_not_exist: <bool>\n      max_chains: <int>\n      taint_flow:\n        source_taint: <str>\n        prohibited_sink_zone: <str>\n        allowed_sanitizers: <list[str]>\n```\nSpec.md §6 (Internal representation), §9 (Behavior map JSON), §10 (Sketch output), §11 (Slicing behavior) don't describe this schema. ADR-0017 (taint propagation) hints at the taint_flow sub-fields but the constraint envelope is not spec'd. Consumers writing claim YAMLs have no spec-level reference; they must read load_claims() in verify_claims.py. INV-luhur spec-narrative face.\n\nFix-path candidate: a dedicated §10b or §12 in spec.md formalizing the claims schema; OR an authoritative `docs/claims-schema.md` with JSON Schema.\n\n**F31.6 — sketch --input cannot complete on cached substrate within 5 minutes; --help claim \"Use cached results file instead of re-analyzing (faster)\" is materially incorrect for substrate at this codebase scale.**\n\n```\nsketch --input bm.json -t 8000 --no-secret-scan --no-progress  → timeout at 5m (exit 124)\nsketch --input bm.json -t 2000 --no-secret-scan --no-progress  → timeout at 3m (exit 124)\nsketch /home/jgstern_agent/hypergumbo/packages/hypergumbo-tracker -t 4000 --no-secret-scan  → completes in <90s, produces 415-line Markdown\n```\n\nThe --input path is materially slower than the fresh-analysis path (which DOES complete). For a 140MB substrate / 34253-node graph, sketch --input apparently does O(repo-size × something) redundant work that the fresh path bypasses. INV-papuj already umbrellas \"sketch read-path does redundant computation when authoritative data is on disk\"; pass-31 supplies the substrate-level quantification: not just \"slower,\" but \"doesn't complete within 5 minutes at any token budget tested.\"\n\nThe --help claim \"faster\" is the INV-rotup-family drift: --help text doesn't match behavior at codebase scale.\n\nFix-path candidate: profile sketch --input on this substrate; the perf gap suggests an O(N²) or O(N × M) loop that's avoided in the fresh path; OR fix the --help text to reflect the perf reality.\n\n**F31.7 — substrate subcommand performance profile.**\n\n| Tier | Subcommand | --input wall-clock |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fast (<3s) | cache-status | 0.28s |\n| | routes | 1.17s |\n| | search | 1.19s |\n| | test-coverage | 2.23s |\n| | verify-claims (valid YAML) | 2.47s |\n| | explain Symbol | 2.85s |\n| | io-boundaries | 2.86s |\n| | symbols (--limit 200 default) | 2.92s |\n| Medium (3-15s) | compact | 5.00s |\n| | symbols --all | 12.57s |\n| Slow (60s+) | dead-code-maybe | ~90s |\n| Won't-complete (>5m) | sketch (--input, any token budget) | timeout exit 124 |\n\n`symbols --all` scales 4.3× the default (12.57s vs 2.92s) — proportional and expected. Slow-cluster (dead-code-maybe, sketch) is 1-2 orders of magnitude slower than fast-cluster. Documentation should set this expectation; users currently have no signal about per-subcommand wall-clock characteristics. Maps to existing umbrellas WI-tuduf (bash analyzer 15x slower per-file) for per-pass analysis and INV-papuj for sketch redundant computation.\n\nNo status change. Pass-31 supplies 4 distinct spec-drift faces — production-functions rule documentation gap, verify-claims schema documentation gap, sketch --input perf claim contradiction, and the broader subcommand performance profile that should inform user documentation.\n\nProbe artifacts: /tmp/pass31_probeA.py + outputs; sketch tests in /tmp/p31_sketch*.md."  # 8f71
- op: discuss  # 24e4
  at: '2026-06-02T08:00:53Z'  # 24e4
  by: agent  # 24e4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 24e4
  clock: 11  # 24e4
  nonce: '24e4'  # 24e4
  message: "Pass-32 spec-narrative & meta-layer drift batch — 6 findings (F32.2 files_analyzed non-determinism / F32.3 three linker counts / F32.4 catalog pass-set vs substrate / F32.5 LINKERS.md gap / F32.11 nodes_summary naming / F32.12 compact selective-truncation undocumented).\n\nPass-32 exercised pass-31's queued probe class \"hypergumbo catalog/config vs spec analyzer & linker lists\" plus the related determinism probe (fresh `hypergumbo run` vs frozen `/tmp/hg-dogfood-s21/bm.json`). Findings:\n\n**F32.2 — `AR.files_analyzed` is non-deterministic across runs on 18/84 ARs.** Frozen substrate vs fresh re-run on identical source tree:\n- javascript: fa 9→1 (-8)   sql: fa 1→9 (+8)\n- json: fa 9→1 (-8)         properties: fa 0→9 (+9)\n- toml: fa 9→0 (-9)         make: fa 0→3 (+3)\n- gitignore: fa 3→9 (+6)    xml: fa 1→0 (-1)\n- yaml_ansible: fa 0→1 (+1) java: fa 1→0 (-1)\nThe 10 ARs above plus 8 more swap which files they each claim. Graph output is **identical** (34253 nodes, 109915 edges, ID sets match, edge-triplet sets match), so the swap is among absent-language analyzers that produce zero nodes regardless of which files they get assigned. Mechanism candidate: dict-iteration-order / first-claim-wins in the file router. Symptom is benign at the graph level but `AR.files_analyzed` becomes provenance noise — re-runs report different per-pass file counts. Same family as INV-bikaj (passes-emit-zero), but at the meta-layer instead of the structural-output layer. The pass-31 yield function held: this probe was virgin and produced a fresh finding immediately.\n\n**F32.3 — Three documentation surfaces give three linker counts.** spec.md §7 line 405 says \"the full catalog of 45 linkers grouped by subcategory\"; LINKERS.md's catalogue table has 46 entries and its bottom-line text says \"Count: 46 linkers — Protocol 13, Bridge 10, Framework 17, Infrastructure 6\"; `hypergumbo catalog` reports 58 linker-named passes (54 if the 5 view_template subfamilies fold to 1 logical linker). Three sources, three counts. After name-normalization (catalog hyphens → underscores, `_linker` suffix strip): 45 normalized linker names overlap with LINKERS.md's 46; LINKERS.md is missing one face (`subprocess_cli` is `subprocess-linker` in catalog) and catalog has 13 entries not in LINKERS.md (see F32.5). Drift is real: spec.md cites the smallest number, catalog has the largest, LINKERS.md sits in between but is also stale.\n\n**F32.4 — 26 catalog-known passes are not tracked anywhere in substrate; 1 substrate AR is not in catalog.** Substrate has 84 ARs that ran + 66 `limits.skipped_passes` entries = 150 tracked passes. `hypergumbo catalog` reports 175 passes. Gap = 25 entries (after also counting the converse). The catalog-only set:\n```\n{cgo-linker, django-third-party-dispatch-linker, graphql-linker, grpc-linker,\n lua-ffi-linker, matlab, meson, napi-linker, openapi-linker, phoenix-ipc-linker,\n puppet, racket, react-component-linker, robot, ruby-ffi-linker, rust_analyzer,\n scheme, scss, swift-objc-linker, view_template, view_template_django,\n view_template_laravel, view_template_phoenix, view_template_spring,\n vue-component-linker, vue-template-method-linker}\n```\nConversely, substrate has `enclosure-linker` as an AR (it ran) but the catalog doesn't list it. Two-way asymmetry between (a) what the catalog advertises, (b) what `hypergumbo run` decides to attempt, (c) what gets logged in `limits.skipped_passes`. Either the pass registry has more entries than the dispatcher knows about, or the dispatcher silently drops 26 passes without logging. Existing `INV-nanon` and `INV-bikaj` cover \"passes execute and emit no edges\"; pass-32 surfaces the dual face \"passes registered but never reach AR/skipped\".\n\n**F32.5 — 8 linker passes shipped in catalog are absent from LINKERS.md.** After folding view_template subfamilies and normalizing names, LINKERS.md is missing 8 production linkers:\n```\n{controller_routes, router_routes, kafka_streams_dispatch, jackson_dispatch,\n django_orm_dispatch, airflow_framework_dispatch, rust_trait_dispatch,\n inherited_calls}\n```\nLINKERS.md's authoritative \"Count: 46\" line is wrong by at least 8 — actual production set is 54+ (after view_template fold). The cosmetic discrepancy of `subprocess` vs `subprocess_cli` is a minor rename face. Each missing linker is a distinct dispatch / framework / language-pair concern; their absence from LINKERS.md likely means downstream readers (consumers learning what linkers do) miss the corresponding fix-points.\n\n**F32.11 — `compact --help` documents \"summary of omitted symbols\" but the actual top-level output key is `nodes_summary`.** `hypergumbo compact --input … --out …` produces a top-level field `nodes_summary` (introducing 1 new key vs the input bm.json's 22 keys). `compact --help` text reads:\n```\nConverts an existing behavior map to compact form with:\n- Top symbols by centrality coverage (connectivity-aware selection by default)\n- Summary of omitted symbols (bag-of-words, path patterns, kinds)\n- Induced subgraph edges (only edges between included symbols)\n```\n\"Summary of omitted symbols\" implies a field named `summary` or `omitted_summary`. The actual field is `nodes_summary` — and per existing INV-pazur, its `included.count` overstates the actual `len(nodes)` in tiered emission. Two faces on the same field: name doesn't match documentation (F32.11), and the value doesn't match the data (INV-pazur). Sub-pattern (1) same-fact-two-names at help-text vs output-key.\n\n**F32.12 — `compact`'s \"selective truncation per top-level field\" rule is undocumented.** `hypergumbo compact` on the substrate produces a structurally near-identical bm.json (22 of 22 input top-level keys, +1 new `nodes_summary`). Of the 6 array-typed top-level fields:\n- nodes: 34253 → 100 (0.29%)        truncated\n- edges: 109915 → 370 (0.34%)       truncated\n- entrypoints: 104 → 33 (31.73%)    truncated (induced filter on symbol_id)\n- features: 16 → 16 (100%)          PRESERVED\n- usage_contexts: 8 → 8 (100%)      PRESERVED\n- analysis_runs: 84 → 84 (100%)     PRESERVED\nThe \"compact form\" promise applies asymmetrically — provenance metadata (analysis_runs, features) is not compacted, only the node/edge/entrypoint graph. This is plausibly correct design (provenance must survive to interpret the compact subgraph), but `--help` says \"Top symbols by centrality coverage\" without specifying which top-level fields are truncated vs preserved. Consumers of the compact output don't know without reading `compact.py:format_tiered_behavior_map` source. INV-luhur spec-narrative drift at the compact-format layer.\n\n**Probe artifacts.** /tmp/p32_probeA.py + p32_probeA2.py (run-vs-frozen diff producing F32.2 + F32.4-related counts); /tmp/p32_probeB.py (catalog vs substrate vs LINKERS.md, producing F32.3-5); /tmp/p32_probeD.py (compact behavior, producing F32.11-12). Spec.md line 405 quoted; LINKERS.md catalogue table parsed; `hypergumbo catalog` and `hypergumbo compact --help` outputs captured.\n\n**No status change.** Pass-32 supplies six face-level discussion entries to INV-luhur covering catalog/config/LINKERS.md/compact spec-narrative drift, plus the files_analyzed non-determinism finding which is INV-luhur scope (writer-contract on AR fields)."  # 24e4
- op: discuss  # 0d40
  at: '2026-06-02T16:12:13Z'  # 0d40
  by: agent  # 0d40
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0d40
  clock: 12  # 0d40
  nonce: 0d40  # 0d40
  message: "Pass-33 batch — spec-narrative + catalog drift (5 findings: F33.5 JSON-output-flag fragmentation across 12 subcommands / F33.6 io_primitives catalog status drift / F33.7 io-boundaries CLI 9× larger than pass-30 / F33.10 typescript no catalog / F33.18 all 16 features attributed to one module).\n\n**F33.5 — JSON-output-flag fragmentation across 12 subcommands.** Pass-30 F30.15 catalogued `config` vs `verify-claims` flag-name divergence; pass-33 surveys all 12 read subcommands and finds **three distinct conventions**:\n```\n                       --format    --json    --input\nslice                  no          no        yes      (writes to --out file as JSON by default)\nexplain                no          no        yes      (stdout text)\nsearch                 no          no        yes      (stdout text)\nroutes                 no          no        yes      (stdout text)\nsymbols                no          no        yes      (stdout text)\nverify-claims          no          yes       yes      (--json switches to JSON)\nconfig                 yes         no        no       (--format {json,yaml,text})\nio-boundaries          no          yes       yes\ntest-coverage          yes         no        yes      (--format {text,json})\ndead-code-maybe        yes         no        yes      (--format {text,json})\ncache-status           no          no        no       (no JSON output)\ncompact                no          no        yes      (writes to --out file as JSON by default)\n```\nThree conventions: (a) `--format {json,yaml,text}` (config, test-coverage, dead-code-maybe — 3); (b) `--json` flag (verify-claims, io-boundaries — 2); (c) neither, but writes JSON to `--out` (slice, compact — 2); (d) neither and only text (explain, search, routes, symbols, cache-status — 5). 7 of 12 subcommands have NO `--format json` flag. Consumers writing scripts must know the convention per-subcommand. WI-gogif scope. Pass-31's F31.5 (`text` vs `claim_text`) and pass-30's F30.15 are sub-faces of this broader fragmentation.\n\n**F33.6 — io_primitives YAML catalog: 11 of 14 marked `in_progress`; only 3 `complete`.** `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/io_primitives/` ships 14 language YAMLs (c, cpp, elixir, erlang, go, haskell, java, javascript, kotlin, objc, python, rust, scala, swift). Status field distribution:\n```\nstatus: complete    — 3   (erlang, python, rust)\nstatus: in_progress — 11  (everything else)\n```\n21% of catalogs declare themselves complete. The in_progress catalogs ship to production but the catalog itself signals partial coverage. Consumer impact: a substrate-side analysis on a Java codebase will use Java's `in_progress` catalog without warning. The catalog's `status` field is a producer-facing signal not a consumer-facing one.\n\n**F33.7 — io-boundaries CLI reports 7891 total_io_edges; substrate has 0 meta.io_boundary entries.** Pass-30 F30.13 reported 847 calls + 0 substrate entries. Pass-33 finds the io-boundaries `--json` output now reports `total_io_edges: 7891` (9.3× larger than pass-30's count). The substrate still carries 0 `meta.io_boundary` and 0 `meta.io_primitive` entries. The 9× growth in io-boundaries count without corresponding substrate growth suggests one of: (a) the CLI's matching algorithm changed and now catches more, (b) pass-30's \"847\" was a different measurement (perhaps only a specific subset), (c) the substrate didn't change but CLI did. Either way, sub-pattern (11) \"CLI recomputes when substrate stamping missing\" is now quantified at substantially higher scale than originally documented. Consumer impact: every `io-boundaries` invocation pays the recomputation cost in lieu of substrate stamping.\n\n**F33.10 — typescript has 0 io_primitives YAML despite substrate having TypeScript nodes.** Substrate emits typescript-language nodes (counted in profile.languages); the io_primitives directory has no `typescript.yaml`. `javascript.yaml` exists but is `in_progress`; whether it covers TS by extension is undocumented. Substrate-detected languages without a complete io_primitives catalog:\n```\nsubstrate languages: ansible, bash, csharp, go, html, java, javascript, json,\n                     markdown, python, rust, solidity, sql, swift, toml,\n                     typescript, wgsl  (17 total)\ncatalog ∩ substrate: go, java, javascript, python, rust, swift  (6 total — and only 3 of these have status=complete)\n```\n13 of 17 substrate languages have NO complete io_primitives catalog. The 21% completeness from F33.6 understates the gap when measured against actual substrate languages.\n\n**F33.18 — All 16 features in the substrate originate from one module (`hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/serve.py`); hypergumbo CLI proper has zero detected features.** Pass-33 compared the substrate's `features` array against the tracker-package substrate (`/tmp/p33_tracker_bm.json`). Both substrates have EXACTLY 16 features; the 16 names in the root substrate are:\n```\nserve.py:101-107:_health:function\nserve.py:110-116:_api_list_items:function\n...\nserve.py:215-250:_ws_handler:function\nserve.py:426-426:GET /health:route\n...\nserve.py:433-433:WS /ws:route\n```\nAll 16 features point at `hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/serve.py` — 8 handler functions + 8 route declarations. **None of hypergumbo's CLI subcommands** (run, slice, compact, symbols, etc.) appear as features. The `features` detection on the hypergumbo CLI codebase is empty; only the tracker package's HTTP routes generate features. Pass-23 NULL \"features (16) cross-resolve at 100%\" confirmed structural validity; pass-33 shows the population is asymmetric — features = \"auto-detected HTTP route handlers\" effectively, not \"named slices of the codebase\" as the spec implies (spec §13). On a non-web codebase, features=[] would be the equivalent finding; on a mixed codebase, features = HTTP-routes-only. INV-luhur spec-vs-substrate-derivation drift at the features layer.\n\n**Probe artifacts.** /tmp/p33_outputA2.log (F33.5 from A.10); /tmp/p33_outputB.log (F33.6 from B.2; F33.7 from B.3+B.4; F33.10 from B.10); /tmp/p33_outputC.log (F33.18 cross-substrate comparison).\n\n**No status change.** Pass-33 supplies five face-level discussion entries to INV-luhur covering JSON-flag fragmentation, io_primitives catalog status drift, io-boundaries 9× growth, typescript catalog gap, and features-attribution narrowness."  # 0d40
